\pard\sa160\b\fs28 Detailed Figure Descriptions\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa160\b\fs24 Fig. 1 — System Architecture for Task-Bound Agent Authorization\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 \f1 \cf2 Fig. 1\f0 \cf0 depicts an example system including a principal device or principal-facing interface, an identity service, an agent registry, a policy engine, an authorization issuer, an agent gateway, one or more protected platform services, a delegation manager, a risk engine, and an audit store.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 The figure should show that a principal submits or approves a task, an agent authenticates to the platform, and the platform issues a delegation artifact that is later enforced by the gateway before the gateway forwards permitted requests to protected services. The figure should also show that audit events and delegation lineage are preserved.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 Suggested drawing callouts:\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab \f1 \cf2 102\f0 \cf0 principal interface\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab \f1 \cf2 104\f0 \cf0 identity service\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab \f1 \cf2 106\f0 \cf0 agent registry\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab \f1 \cf2 108\f0 \cf0 policy engine\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab \f1 \cf2 110\f0 \cf0 authorization issuer\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab \f1 \cf2 112\f0 \cf0 agent gateway\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab \f1 \cf2 114\f0 \cf0 protected services\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab \f1 \cf2 116\f0 \cf0 delegation manager\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab \f1 \cf2 118\f0 \cf0 risk engine\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab \f1 \cf2 120\f0 \cf0 audit subsystem\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa160\b\fs24 Fig. 2 — Delegation Issuance Workflow\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 \f1 \cf2 Fig. 2\f0 \cf0 depicts a flow in which a principal and an agent authenticate, a delegated authorization request is received, policy inputs are evaluated, requested scope is compared against permitted scope, and a short-lived delegation artifact is issued.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 Suggested sequence:\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 1.\tab Principal authenticates\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 2.\tab Agent authenticates\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 3.\tab Request for delegation is submitted\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 4.\tab Policy engine evaluates principal, agent, and requested scope\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 5.\tab Optional trust-tier and classification checks are applied\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 6.\tab Scoped delegation artifact is minted\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 7.\tab Delegation record is stored\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 8.\tab Artifact is returned to the agent runtime\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 The figure should emphasize that the artifact is bound to a specific delegated context rather than broad standing permissions.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa160\b\fs24 Fig. 3 — Runtime Action Enforcement Workflow\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 \f1 \cf2 Fig. 3\f0 \cf0 depicts the runtime path for an agent-initiated action. The agent presents the delegation artifact with an action request. The gateway intercepts the request, validates token integrity and scope, computes a compound authorization decision, and either denies the action or forwards it to a protected service.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 Suggested decision branches:\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab validate signature or integrity\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab validate expiration and revocation status\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab validate action against approved tools and actions\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab validate resource and classification boundaries\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab compute principal, agent, and delegation decisions\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab allow, deny, modify, or escalate\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab record audit event\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 The figure should make clear that enforcement occurs before the target service executes the requested action.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa160\b\fs24 Fig. 4 — Multi-Agent Delegated Authorization Chain\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 \f1 \cf2 Fig. 4\f0 \cf0 depicts a parent agent delegating a subtask to a child agent through issuance of a derivative authorization artifact. The figure should show a root delegation identifier, parent delegation identifier, reduced child scope, and bounded delegation depth.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 Suggested elements:\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab root principal approval\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab parent delegation artifact\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab child delegation request\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab scope-subset validation\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab child delegation artifact\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab child agent execution\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab lineage record written to audit store\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 The figure should emphasize that child scope cannot exceed parent scope and that delegation chains remain traceable.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa160\b\fs24 Fig. 5 — Dynamic Narrowing and Revocation Workflow\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 \f1 \cf2 Fig. 5\f0 \cf0 depicts runtime risk monitoring and revocation. The figure should show an active delegation artifact being monitored by a risk engine or policy update path. Upon detection of an anomaly, policy change, trust-tier downgrade, or explicit revocation, the system marks the artifact as revoked or narrowed and distributes that state to enforcement points.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 Suggested flow:\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 1.\tab Active artifact is in use\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 2.\tab Risk or policy event is detected\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 3.\tab Revocation or narrowing decision is computed\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 4.\tab Artifact state is updated\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 5.\tab Child delegations are revoked or frozen\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 6.\tab Future actions are denied or constrained\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 7.\tab Revocation event is logged\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa160\b\fs24 Fig. 6 — Provenance and Audit Lineage Graph\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 \f1 \cf2 Fig. 6\f0 \cf0 depicts a graph or linked-record structure connecting:\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab principal approval\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab agent registration\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab delegation artifact issuance\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab tool invocation events\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab child delegation events\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab target-service responses\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab output generation\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab revocation or expiration\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 The figure should show that each node is linked by identifiers such as principal ID, agent ID, delegation ID, parent delegation ID, and root delegation ID, allowing reconstruction of the full authorization-to-execution chain.\par
