\pard\sa160\b\fs28 Problem Statement\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 Autonomous and semi-autonomous agents need to act on behalf of users and other principals, but conventional authorization systems are user-centric or service-account-centric. Those systems do not reliably constrain an agent to a specific approved task, delegated scope, runtime context, or downstream delegation boundary.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 This causes several technical problems:\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab privilege amplification when an agent's standing privileges exceed the user's intended authority\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab inability to distinguish user authority from agent authority in a single runtime decision\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab uncontrolled propagation of permissions across sub-agents\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab weak linkage between approval, delegated token issuance, executed actions, and resulting outputs\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab limited ability to revoke or narrow authorization during live agent execution\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa160\b\fs28 Core Invention\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 The invention issues a task-bound, machine-readable authorization artifact for an agent acting on behalf of a principal and enforces that artifact at runtime for each attempted action. Authorization is evaluated using a compound decision that includes at least principal authority, agent authority, and delegation authority. Child delegation is supported through derivative artifacts that cannot exceed parent scope. Audit and provenance records link approval, issuance, execution, delegation, and revocation.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa160\b\fs28 Key Novel Features\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab double-hop or compound authorization combining principal, agent, and delegated scope\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab short-lived delegation artifacts bound to a user-agent-session or task context\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab runtime gateway enforcement for each tool or service invocation\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab reduced-scope child delegation for multi-agent workflows\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab per-hierarchy classification caps and resource constraints\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab configurable maximum delegation depth\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab dynamic narrowing or revocation during execution\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab provenance linking approval, delegation chain, actions, and outputs\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa160\b\fs28 High-Level Workflow\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 1.\tab A principal authenticates or otherwise approves a task.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 2.\tab An agent authenticates using platform-recognized credentials.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 3.\tab The platform evaluates policies using principal attributes, agent attributes, and requested delegated scope.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 4.\tab The platform issues a bounded delegation artifact.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 5.\tab The agent presents the artifact when invoking tools or protected services.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 6.\tab A gateway or enforcement layer validates each request against the delegated scope and computes a compound authorization decision.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 7.\tab The platform optionally issues narrowed child artifacts for downstream agents.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 8.\tab The platform records provenance and supports revocation, expiration, or narrowing.\par
\pard\sa120\par
