\pard\sa160\b\fs28 Provisional-Style Draft Text\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa160\b\fs24 Title\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 Systems and Methods for Task-Bound Authorization of Autonomous Software Agents\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa160\b\fs24 Technical Field\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 The disclosure relates to computer security, identity and access management, distributed systems, and runtime control of autonomous or semi-autonomous software agents.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa160\b\fs24 Problem Addressed\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 Autonomous agents increasingly perform actions on behalf of users, tenants, services, and other principals. Existing user-centric and service-account-centric authorization models do not adequately constrain agents to a particular approved task, execution scope, or delegation boundary. This can result in privilege amplification, overbroad tool access, poor containment of downstream delegation, and limited forensic traceability.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa160\b\fs24 Summary of the Invention\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 In one embodiment, a platform receives a task request associated with a principal and identifies an agent authorized to act on the principal's behalf. The platform evaluates principal permissions, agent permissions, and explicitly delegated scope using a compound policy decision. The platform issues a short-lived authorization artifact that is bound to the delegated context and defines a bounded permission envelope for the agent.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 At runtime, a gateway or similar mediation layer intercepts each attempted tool or service invocation by the agent and validates the invocation against the authorization artifact. If the action is within scope and the compound decision resolves to allow, the request is forwarded to a protected service. If not, the request is denied, modified, or escalated.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 In some embodiments, a first agent delegates a subtask to a second agent only through issuance of a derivative authorization artifact that is equal to or narrower than the parent artifact. In some embodiments, the platform dynamically narrows or revokes authorization in response to anomaly detection, policy changes, trust-tier changes, or other execution conditions. The platform also records provenance linking approval, authorization issuance, delegation events, executed actions, and resulting outputs.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa160\b\fs24 Representative Embodiments\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa160\b\fs22 Embodiment 1: Compound Authorization\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 Authorization is computed from the intersection of:\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab authority of the principal\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab authority of the acting agent\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab explicitly delegated scope\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 This model prevents either the principal or the agent from unilaterally expanding effective permission.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa160\b\fs22 Embodiment 2: Delegation Artifact\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 The system issues a machine-readable artifact containing one or more of:\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab principal identifier\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab agent identifier\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab tenant identifier\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab delegation identifier\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab root delegation identifier\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab parent delegation identifier\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab approved tools\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab approved actions\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab maximum action tier\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab classification caps\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab resource constraints\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab declared purpose\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab session or conversation identifier\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab issued-at and expiration timestamps\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab chain depth and chain lineage fields\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa160\b\fs22 Embodiment 3: Runtime Enforcement\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 Each agent action is intercepted before execution. The system validates:\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab integrity and authenticity of the delegation artifact\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab expiration and revocation status\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab allowed tool and action scope\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab resource and classification boundaries\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab compound authorization outcome\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa160\b\fs22 Embodiment 4: Child Delegation\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 The system allows multi-agent workflows by issuing child artifacts that remain within a subset of the parent scope. In some embodiments:\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab tools can only be removed, not added\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab action sensitivity can only narrow\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab resource access can only narrow\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab classification caps can only narrow\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab delegation depth is bounded\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa160\b\fs22 Embodiment 5: Revocation and Provenance\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa120 The system supports revocation, narrowing, and post-hoc verification. Audit records may connect:\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab principal approval\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab agent registration\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab policy evaluation\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab artifact issuance\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab runtime invocation\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab child delegation\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab revocation\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab generated outputs\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\sa160\b\fs24 Example Use Cases\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab A research agent can search internal documents and produce summaries but cannot export source material.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab A coding agent can inspect and modify specified repositories but cannot perform destructive actions above its delegated action tier.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab A support agent can query records for a particular tenant and case but cannot access unrelated customer data.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360 \bullet	ab A planning agent can create a narrowed child delegation for an execution agent.\par
\pard\sa120\par
